            <ArticleTitle>Age as a determinant of cardiopulmonary resuscitation outcome in the coronary care unit.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>634-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine whether age is associated with the outcome of cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR) in the coronary care unit (CCU).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Retrospective chart review.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The coronary care units of two Canadian tertiary care teaching hospitals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two hundred sixty-four coronary care unit patients undergoing cardiopulmonary resuscitation between January 1, 1985 and June 30, 1992.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was no significant difference in survival to discharge after CPR between patients less than 70 years of age (17.0%) and patients 70 years of age and older (17.2%) (odds ratio = 0.99; 95% confidence interval = 0.46, 1.80). Patients 70 years of age and older who survived to discharge after CPR had significantly greater lengths of stay (28.1 vs 19.3 days, P = .008).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Age was not associated with a difference in survival to discharge after CPR in the CCU, although a clinically significant difference could not be excluded because of limited power.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
